@page "/components/datagrid"
@page "/components/data-grid"
@page "/components/datagrid/{Topic}"
@inherits AppComponentBase

<PageOutlet Url="components/datagrid"
            Title="DataGrid"
            Description="BitDataGrid displays an information-rich collection of items with sorting, searching, filtering, paging, grouping, selection, inline editing, virtualization and Excel/CSV export." />

<DemoPage Name="DataGrid"
          HasTopics
          Topic="@Topic"
          SecondaryNames="@(["DataTable", "TreeGrid", "TreeList"])"
          Description="BitDataGrid displays an information-rich data table and gives people the tools to work through it: sorting, quick search and per-column filters, paging, grouping with aggregates, selection, inline editing, resizable/reorderable/frozen columns, master-detail and tree rows, keyboard cell navigation, row and column virtualization, and CSV/Excel export. Bind a list, an IQueryable executed at the source, or a callback for server-side paging or infinite scrolling. Use a data grid when information density is critical."
          Parameters="componentParameters"
          PublicMembers="componentPublicMembers"
          CssVariables="componentCssVariables"
          SubClasses="componentSubClasses"
          SubEnums="componentSubEnums"
          GitHubExtrasUrl="DataGrid/BitDataGrid.razor"
          GitHubDemoUrl="Extras/DataGrid/BitDataGridDemo.razor">
    <NotesTemplate>
        <BitText>
            To use this component, you need to install the
            <BitLink Href="https://www.nuget.org/packages/Bit.BlazorUI.Extras" Target="_blank">
                <BitTag Reversed
                        Text="Bit.BlazorUI.Extras"
                        Color="BitColor.SecondaryBackground"
                        IconName="@BitIconName.NavigateExternalInline" />
            </BitLink>
            NuGet package, as described in the Optional steps of the
            <BitLink Href="/getting-started">Getting started</BitLink> page.
        </BitText>
        <br />
        <BitText>
            BitDataGrid is generic over its row type: <b>TItem</b> is inferred from <b>Items</b> (or from what <b>OnRead</b> /
            <b>OnLoadMore</b> return), and every <b>BitDataGridColumn</b> inside takes it from the grid. Name it yourself
            (<code>TItem="Product"</code>) where the compiler cannot infer it - a grid captured with <code>@@ref</code>, or a column
            handed a method group such as <code>CellStyleSelector="StockStyleFor"</code>. The grid picks its data mode from
            what it is given: a list or array is sorted, filtered, grouped and paged in memory; an <b>IQueryable</b> is
            translated into a query run at the source; <b>OnRead</b> hands every page to your code; <b>OnLoadMore</b> scrolls
            without end; <b>ChildrenSelector</b>, <b>ChildrenProvider</b> or <b>OnLoadChildren</b> turn the rows into a tree.
            Grouping, the pager and some other features are available only in some of these modes - each section says which.
        </BitText>
    </NotesTemplate>
    <Examples>

        <DemoTopic Title="Overview"
                   Description="Binding the rows and declaring the columns, sorting, the filter row and paging, quick search, row selection, and keyboard cell navigation with clipboard copy.">
            <DemoExample Title="Basic &amp; sorting" RazorCode="@example1RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example1CsharpCode" Id="example1">
                <div>
                    Bind <b>Items</b> and declare the columns; <b>Property</b> binds a column with a typed selector. A header click sorts
                    ascending, descending, then unsorted (<b>AllowUnsorted="false"</b> drops the last step), and Ctrl/⌘ or Shift+click adds a
                    column to the sort. <b>AriaLabel</b> names the grid for screen readers - or <b>AriaLabelledBy</b>, pointing at a visible heading.
                    <br /><br />
                    The columns go inside <b>Columns</b>, as here, or straight into the grid's child content; when both are given, both render.
                    <b>Property</b> reaches nested members too (<code>p =&gt; p.Address.City</code>) and takes precedence over <b>Field</b>, the
                    same binding by name. A bound column gets its <b>ColumnId</b> from the property path and its <b>Title</b> from the property
                    name, humanized, unless you set them; the id is what the sort, filter, group and state APIs refer to.
                    <b>Format</b> is a .NET format string applied in the current culture (<code>C2</code>, <code>N1</code>,
                    <code>yyyy-MM-dd</code>). <b>Align</b> places the header and cells: <i>Start</i> (the default) and <i>End</i> follow the
                    reading direction, <i>Left</i> and <i>Right</i> stay on their side of the screen, <i>Center</i> centers, and any other
                    <b>BitTextAlign</b> value is treated as Start. <b>Width</b> takes any CSS width; a column without one shares the space left over.
                    <br /><br />
                    Sorting is on by default: <b>Sortable="false"</b> turns it off for the grid, or for one column when set on it.
                    <b>MultiSort</b> (default <code>true</code>) is what lets the modifier click add a column rather than replace the sort, and a
                    column's <b>SortDescendingFirst</b> makes its first click sort descending. A column bound to nothing (a template-only column)
                    sorts only through <b>SortBy</b> - see <BitLink Href="/components/datagrid/editing#example8">Templates</BitLink>.
                    Without <b>AriaLabel</b> or <b>AriaLabelledBy</b> the grid is named by <b>Strings.GridLabel</b> ("Data grid"), and
                    <b>AriaDescribedBy</b> adds a description read after the name.
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitDataGrid Items="@basicProducts" Height="430px" AriaLabel="Products">
                    <Columns>
                        <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Id" Title="ID" Width="70px" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                        <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Name" Width="220px" />
                        <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Category" />
                        <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Price" Format="C2" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                        <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Stock" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                        <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Rating" Format="N1" Align="BitTextAlign.End" AllowUnsorted="false" />
                    </Columns>
                </BitDataGrid>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="Filtering &amp; paging" RazorCode="@example2RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example2CsharpCode" Id="example2">
                <div>
                    <b>Filterable</b> adds a filter row whose editor follows each column's type and filters as you type (<b>FilterDebounce</b>).
                    <b>FilterOperators</b> adds an operator picker
                    (including <i>Is blank</i>, and <i>Is any of</i> taking a comma-separated list), which a column can opt out of.
                    A column's <b>FilterTemplate</b> replaces its editor - here a multi-select applying <b>In</b>. <b>Pageable</b> pages the rows,
                    and <b>ShowToolbar</b> shows <i>Clear filters</i> while a filter is on. These columns bind by <b>Field</b> name.
                    <br /><br />
                    Each column's editor comes from its <b>DataType</b>, read off the property type while it is <i>Auto</i>: a text box for
                    text (matching <i>Contains</i>, ignoring case), a number box for numbers, a date picker for dates, and a dropdown for
                    booleans and enums (all matching <i>Equals</i>, a date the whole day). Set <b>DataType</b> to pick another. The filters of
                    several columns combine with AND, and every change of a filter goes back to the first page.
                    <b>FilterDebounce</b> (300 ms) only delays the text and number boxes: <code>0</code> applies each keystroke and a
                    negative value waits for Enter or for the box to lose focus; the pickers and dropdowns apply on every change.
                    <b>Filterable</b> and <b>FilterOperators</b> on a column override the grid's (ID has no filter, Supplier no operator
                    picker), and a column bound to nothing has no filter.
                    <br /><br />
                    The <b>FilterTemplate</b> context carries the column's filter (<b>Operator</b>, <b>Value</b>, <b>IsActive</b>), the
                    <b>Label</b> to give the control as its accessible name, the grid's <b>Disabled</b>, and <b>ApplyAsync</b>,
                    <b>ApplyRangeAsync</b> (a half-open range: from inclusive, to exclusive) and <b>ClearAsync</b>. They go through the same
                    path as the built-in editors, so <b>OnFilterChange</b>, Clear filters and the announcements follow; an empty value or
                    an empty set clears the filter.
                    <br /><br />
                    <b>PageSize</b> (20) is the rows per page and <b>PageSizeOptions</b> (10, 20, 50, 100) the sizes the pager offers.
                    Paging is set aside while the rows are grouped, in a tree and with <b>OnLoadMore</b>, which show every row they hold.
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitDataGrid Items="@filterProducts" Height="430px"
                             Filterable="true" FilterOperators="true"
                             Pageable="true" PageSize="10" ShowToolbar="true">
                    <BitDataGridColumn Field="Id" Title="ID" Width="70px" Align="BitTextAlign.End" Filterable="false" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Field="Name" Width="220px" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Field="Category">
                        <FilterTemplate>
                            <BitDropdown TItem="BitDropdownItem<string>" TValue="string" Items="categoryFilterItems"
                                         MultiSelect Size="BitSize.Small" Placeholder="All"
                                         AriaLabel="@context.Label" Disabled="context.Disabled"
                                         Values="@(context.Value as IEnumerable<string?>)"
                                         ValuesChanged="values => context.ApplyAsync(BitDataGridFilterOperator.In, values?.ToList())" />
                        </FilterTemplate>
                    </BitDataGridColumn>
                    <BitDataGridColumn Field="Supplier" FilterOperators="false" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Field="Price" Format="C2" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Field="ReleaseDate" Title="Released" Format="yyyy-MM-dd" />
                </BitDataGrid>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="Quick search" RazorCode="@example3RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example3CsharpCode" Id="example3">
                <div>
                    <b>ShowSearchBox</b> searches every column at once, matching the text each cell shows; <b>Searchable="false"</b> leaves a
                    column out. <b>SearchText</b> is two-way bindable, and <b>SearchDebounce</b> (300 ms) turns a burst of typing into one search.
                    <br /><br />
                    The match ignores case and is made against each cell's formatted text, so a price is found as it is shown
                    ("1,234.50", not "1234.5"). The search narrows the rows on top of the column filters, and a new term goes back to the
                    first page. By default a column is searched when it has a value - a <b>Property</b> / <b>Field</b>, or an
                    <b>ExportValue</b>; a template-only column without one never matches. Setting <b>SearchText</b> applies the term just
                    as typing would, and the box itself only renders with <b>ShowSearchBox</b>, so the bound value can also drive a search
                    field of your own; <b>SearchAsync</b> does the same from code and <b>ActiveSearch</b> reads it. <code>0</code> for
                    <b>SearchDebounce</b> searches on every keystroke - fine in memory, one request per character against
                    <b>OnRead</b> or an IQueryable.
                    <br /><br />
                    The other data modes: <b>OnRead</b> and <b>OnLoadMore</b> receive the term as the request's <b>Search</b> and match it
                    themselves; an IQueryable turns it into <i>Contains</i> conditions over its text columns, run at the source; a tree
                    prunes itself to the matching branches; a lazily loaded tree (<b>ChildrenProvider</b>, <b>OnLoadChildren</b>) shows no
                    search box, since it cannot see the children it has not loaded.
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitDataGrid Items="@searchProducts" Height="430px"
                             ShowSearchBox="true" @bind-SearchText="searchTerm"
                             Pageable="true" PageSize="10">
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Id" Title="ID" Width="70px" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Name" Width="220px" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Category" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Supplier" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Price" Format="C2" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Stock" Align="BitTextAlign.End" Searchable="false" />
                </BitDataGrid>
                <br />
                <BitText>@(string.IsNullOrEmpty(searchTerm) ? "No search term." : $"Searching for \"{searchTerm}\".")</BitText>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="Selection" RazorCode="@example4RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example4CsharpCode" Id="example4">
                <div>
                    <b>SelectionMode</b> picks a row click (Single, which makes the cells navigable so Space selects too) or checkboxes with a
                    select-all box (Multiple); Shift+click selects a range.
                    <b>SelectedItems</b> is two-way bindable and tracked by <b>KeyField</b>, and <b>IsRowSelectionDisabled</b> locks rows out -
                    discontinued products here. <b>RowHeader</b> makes Name the header of its row: screen readers announce it from row to row,
                    and it names each checkbox.
                    <br /><br />
                    <b>SelectionMode</b> defaults to <i>None</i>. In <i>Single</i> mode a row click selects that row in place of the
                    previous one, and Space on its focused cell toggles it, so only the keyboard clears it; while a row is being edited,
                    clicks select nothing. In <i>Multiple</i> mode a row click selects nothing - the checkboxes (and Space) do - and
                    Shift+click on a checkbox (or Shift+Space) applies the clicked row's new state to every row between it and the last one
                    clicked; the header box selects or clears the rows of the current page, and
                    Ctrl+A or <b>SelectAllAsync</b> every row of the filtered view (only the loaded ones with <b>OnRead</b>, an IQueryable or
                    <b>OnLoadMore</b>). <b>ClearSelectionAsync</b> clears it. A click on a control inside a cell - a button, a link, a field -
                    selects nothing.
                    <br /><br />
                    Without <b>KeyField</b> rows are matched by reference, so a selection is lost when the source hands over new instances
                    of the same rows; with it the selection survives a refresh. Rows <b>IsRowSelectionDisabled</b> rejects show a disabled
                    checkbox and are skipped by every select-all. In Single mode only the first of the <b>SelectedItems</b> given is taken.
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitStack Horizontal Wrap VerticalAlign="BitAlignment.Center">
                    <BitButton Variant="@(selectionMode == BitSelectionMode.Single ? BitVariant.Fill : BitVariant.Outline)"
                               OnClick="SelectSingleMode">Single</BitButton>
                    <BitButton Variant="@(selectionMode == BitSelectionMode.Multiple ? BitVariant.Fill : BitVariant.Outline)"
                               OnClick="() => selectionMode = BitSelectionMode.Multiple">Multiple</BitButton>
                    <BitText>@selectedProducts.Count selected</BitText>
                </BitStack>
                <br />
                <BitDataGrid Items="@selectionProducts" Height="420px" KeyField="p => p.Id"
                             SelectionMode="selectionMode" @bind-SelectedItems="selectedProducts"
                             IsRowSelectionDisabled="p => p.Discontinued"
                             Pageable="true" PageSize="10">
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Id" Title="ID" Width="70px" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Name" Width="220px" RowHeader="true" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Category" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Price" Format="C2" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Discontinued" Align="BitTextAlign.Center" />
                </BitDataGrid>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="Keyboard &amp; clipboard" RazorCode="@example5RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example5CsharpCode" Id="example5">
                <div>
                    <b>CellNavigation</b> makes the cells a single tab stop (the APG grid pattern): the arrows, Home/End, Ctrl+Home/End and
                    Page Up/Down move between them, Space toggles the row, Shift+Space extends the range and Ctrl+A selects every row - so the
                    row checkboxes leave the tab order.
                    With <b>ClipboardCopy</b>, Ctrl/⌘+C copies the selected rows as tab-separated text that pastes into Excel as columns.
                    <br /><br />
                    Cell navigation is also on, whatever <b>CellNavigation</b> says, for Single <b>SelectionMode</b> and for an editable grid in
                    Cell <b>EditMode</b>. With it off, no cell takes the focus and the grid is announced as a plain table (a tree stays a
                    <i>treegrid</i>). In an editable grid Enter or F2 opens the focused cell's editor, typing into a text or number cell opens it
                    with what was typed, and Delete deletes the row (see
                    <BitLink Href="/components/datagrid/editing#example6">Inline editing</BitLink>). With <b>VirtualizeColumns</b> the arrows
                    only reach the columns that are rendered.
                    <br /><br />
                    Copying takes the selected rows, or the focused row when nothing is selected, with a header line of the column titles.
                    A cell is copied as it is formatted, a column's <b>ExportValue</b> replaces its value, and the columns left out of exports
                    (<b>Exportable="false"</b>) are left out here too; row numbers never are copied. Ctrl/⌘+C needs <b>CellNavigation</b>, since
                    it is pressed on a focused cell; <b>CopyToClipboardAsync</b> copies from code (the buttons above) and returns how many rows
                    it copied - <code>0</code> where the browser offers no clipboard. A disabled grid still moves and copies.
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitStack Horizontal Wrap VerticalAlign="BitAlignment.Center">
                    <BitButton OnClick="SelectAllRows">Select all</BitButton>
                    <BitButton OnClick="ClearRowSelection" Variant="BitVariant.Outline">Clear selection</BitButton>
                    <BitButton OnClick="CopySelection" Disabled="clipboardSelection.Count is 0">Copy selection</BitButton>
                    <BitText>@clipboardStatus</BitText>
                </BitStack>
                <br />
                <BitDataGrid @ref="clipboardGrid" TItem="Product" Items="@clipboardProducts" Height="430px" KeyField="p => p.Id"
                             SelectionMode="BitSelectionMode.Multiple" @bind-SelectedItems="clipboardSelection"
                             CellNavigation="true" ClipboardCopy="true">
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Id" Title="ID" Width="70px" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Name" Width="220px" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Category" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Price" Format="C2" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Stock" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                </BitDataGrid>
            </DemoExample>
        </DemoTopic>

        <DemoTopic Title="Editing, grouping & templates" Route="editing"
                   Description="Inline row and cell editing with validation, grouping with group and footer aggregates, cell, header and footer templates with custom sort keys, and master-detail rows.">
            <DemoExample Title="Inline editing" RazorCode="@example6RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example6CsharpCode" Id="example6">
                <div>
                    <b>Editable</b> adds a command column and <b>NewItemFactory</b> an <i>Add</i> button. Editors follow each column's type,
                    edits are buffered until saved, and <b>Validate</b> blocks an invalid value (clear a name, or enter a negative price).
                    <b>EditMode</b> edits the whole row (Save/Cancel) or one cell at a time: Enter, F2 or a double-click opens it, and typing
                    into a text or number cell opens it with what you type; Enter, Tab or leaving it saves; Esc cancels; Delete removes the focused row. A row double-click here calls <b>BeginEdit</b> via <b>OnRowDoubleClick</b>.
                    <br /><br />
                    The grid edits the row object but never your list: the built-in editors write into a buffer, and only Save writes it
                    to the row and raises <b>OnRowSave</b> - persist the change there. Cancel (or Esc) drops the buffer and raises
                    <b>OnRowCancel</b>. <b>OnRowCreate</b> hands over the row <b>NewItemFactory</b> built as it opens for editing, and
                    <b>OnRowDelete</b> the row to delete: the grid only deselects it, so remove it from <b>Items</b> yourself. One row is edited
                    at a time, and while it is open, row clicks neither select nor expand anything.
                    <br /><br />
                    A column is editable when the grid (or its own <b>Editable</b>) says so and it is bound to a property with a setter;
                    <code>Editable="false"</code> keeps ID read-only here, and template-only columns are never edited. The editor follows
                    <b>DataType</b>: a text box, a number box, a date picker, or a dropdown for booleans and enums. A value that cannot be
                    converted shows <b>Strings.InvalidValueError</b>. <b>Validate</b> receives the row and the converted value and returns an
                    error message, or <code>null</code> to accept it; while any column has an error, saving is refused and the message
                    shows under its editor. A column's <b>EditTemplate</b> replaces the built-in editor with your own markup; it binds
                    straight to the row rather than to the buffer, and Cancel restores the values the row had when the edit began.
                    <br /><br />
                    Cell mode turns cell navigation on, and Tab saves and moves to the next editable cell. From code:
                    <b>BeginEdit</b> / <b>BeginEditAsync</b> (a row, or a cell by column id), <b>CommitEditAsync</b>,
                    <b>CancelEditAsync</b>, <b>AddNewRowAsync</b> and <b>DeleteRowAsync</b>; <b>EditingItem</b> and <b>EditingColumnId</b>
                    tell what is open. A row type that is a struct cannot be edited, since the grid would only change a copy.
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitStack Horizontal Wrap VerticalAlign="BitAlignment.Center">
                    <BitButton Variant="@(editMode == BitDataGridEditMode.Row ? BitVariant.Fill : BitVariant.Outline)"
                               OnClick="() => editMode = BitDataGridEditMode.Row">Row</BitButton>
                    <BitButton Variant="@(editMode == BitDataGridEditMode.Cell ? BitVariant.Fill : BitVariant.Outline)"
                               OnClick="() => editMode = BitDataGridEditMode.Cell">Cell</BitButton>
                </BitStack>
                <br />
                <BitDataGrid @ref="editGrid" TItem="Product" Items="@editProducts" Height="460px" KeyField="p => p.Id"
                             Editable="true" EditMode="editMode" NewItemFactory="CreateProduct" CellNavigation="true"
                             OnRowSave="OnSave" OnRowCancel="OnCancel" OnRowDelete="OnDelete" OnRowCreate="OnCreate"
                             OnRowDoubleClick="EditOnDoubleClick"
                             Pageable="true" PageSize="10">
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Id" Title="ID" Width="70px" Align="BitTextAlign.End" Editable="false" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Name" Width="200px" Validate="(p, v) => ValidateName(p, v)" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Category" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Price" Format="C2" Align="BitTextAlign.End" Validate="(p, v) => ValidatePrice(p, v)" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Stock" Align="BitTextAlign.End" Validate="(p, v) => ValidateStock(p, v)" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.ReleaseDate" Title="Released" Format="yyyy-MM-dd" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Discontinued" Align="BitTextAlign.Center" />
                </BitDataGrid>
                <br />
                <BitText>@editStatus</BitText>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="Grouping &amp; aggregates" RazorCode="@example7RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example7CsharpCode" Id="example7">
                <div>
                    <b>Groupable</b> adds a ⊞ toggle to the headers; group by Category, then Supplier, for nested groups. Each group shows its
                    columns' <b>Aggregate</b>, <b>ShowFooter</b> shows the grand totals, and <b>AggregateBy</b> computes a custom one.
                    <b>GroupsInitiallyCollapsed</b> opens the groups closed; <b>ExpandAllGroupsAsync</b> / <b>CollapseAllGroupsAsync</b> flip them all.
                    <br /><br />
                    Grouping works on in-memory lists only: it is not offered with <b>OnRead</b>, <b>OnLoadMore</b>, an IQueryable or a
                    tree, and a column needs a bound property to be grouped by. A grouped grid shows every row, so the pager is set aside
                    while it is grouped. <b>Groupable</b> on a column overrides the grid's. From code, <b>GroupByAsync</b> adds a level,
                    <b>UngroupAsync</b> removes one and <b>ClearGroupsAsync</b> all of them; <b>OnGroupChange</b> reports the new grouping.
                    Expand-all and collapse-all also hold for groups built later, after a regrouping or new data.
                    <br /><br />
                    <b>Aggregate</b> picks a built-in function - <i>Sum</i>, <i>Average</i> (of the numeric values), <i>Count</i> (of the rows),
                    <i>Min</i> or <i>Max</i> (of the non-null values) - and <b>AggregateBy</b> takes the group's (or the whole view's) rows
                    and returns any value, winning over <b>Aggregate</b>. The result is formatted with <b>AggregateFormat</b>, or else the
                    column's <b>Format</b>. The group header shows the aggregates beside its title and count; <b>ShowFooter</b> adds a
                    footer row with the same aggregates over every row of the view, whatever the page.
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitStack Horizontal Wrap VerticalAlign="BitAlignment.Center">
                    <BitButton OnClick="ExpandAllGroups">Expand all groups</BitButton>
                    <BitButton OnClick="CollapseAllGroups" Variant="BitVariant.Outline">Collapse all groups</BitButton>
                </BitStack>
                <br />
                <BitDataGrid @ref="groupGrid" TItem="Product" Items="@groupProducts" Height="500px"
                             Groupable="true" ShowFooter="true">
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Id" Title="ID" Width="70px" Align="BitTextAlign.End" Groupable="false" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Name" Width="200px" Groupable="false" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Category" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Supplier" AggregateBy="rows => DistinctSuppliers(rows)" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Price" Format="C2" Align="BitTextAlign.End"
                                       Aggregate="BitDataGridAggregateType.Sum" Groupable="false" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Stock" Align="BitTextAlign.End"
                                       Aggregate="BitDataGridAggregateType.Average" AggregateFormat="N0" Groupable="false" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Rating" Format="N1" Align="BitTextAlign.End"
                                       Aggregate="BitDataGridAggregateType.Max" Groupable="false" />
                </BitDataGrid>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="Templates" RazorCode="@example8RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example8CsharpCode" Id="example8">
                <div>
                    <b>Template</b>, <b>HeaderTemplate</b> and <b>FooterTemplate</b> render any markup. A template-only column sorts by its
                    <b>SortBy</b> key, and <b>Comparer</b> replaces the ordering itself - Stock sorts in-stock rows first.
                    <br /><br />
                    A <b>Template</b> only changes how a cell looks: a bound column still sorts, filters, searches, groups and exports by its
                    value, so Rating and Stock above keep all of that. <b>FooterTemplate</b> receives the column's
                    <b>BitDataGridAggregateResult</b> (<b>Value</b>, <b>FormattedValue</b>, <b>Type</b>), so it renders only where the column
                    has an <b>Aggregate</b> or <b>AggregateBy</b> and the footer is on. A template owns its markup, so the cell tooltips of
                    <b>ShowCellTooltips</b> are not added to it.
                    <br /><br />
                    A template-only column (no <b>Property</b> or <b>Field</b>, like Value) has no value of its own: give it a
                    <b>ColumnId</b>, since its id is otherwise generated, and know that it is not filtered, grouped, searched or exported
                    unless you give it an <b>ExportValue</b> (see <BitLink Href="/components/datagrid/data#example21">Export</BitLink>).
                    <b>SortBy</b> and <b>Comparer</b> run in memory only: an IQueryable sorts a bound column by its property and cannot sort
                    a template-only one, and <b>OnRead</b> / <b>OnLoadMore</b> receive the column id to sort by - a template-only column
                    takes part there with <b>ServerSortable</b> and a <b>ColumnId</b>. <b>SortBy</b> on a bound column replaces its value
                    as the sort key.
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitDataGrid Items="@templateProducts" Height="470px" ShowFooter="true">
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Name" Width="220px">
                        <HeaderTemplate>📦 Product</HeaderTemplate>
                    </BitDataGridColumn>
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Category" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Rating">
                        <Template Context="p">
                            <span role="img" aria-label="@($"{p.Rating} out of 5")" title="@p.Rating">
                                @for (var i = 0; i < 5; i++)
                                {
                                    <span style="color:@(i < Math.Round(p.Rating, MidpointRounding.AwayFromZero) ? "#f5a623" : "#ccc")">★</span>
                                }
                            </span>
                        </Template>
                    </BitDataGridColumn>
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Price" Format="C2" Align="BitTextAlign.End"
                                       Aggregate="BitDataGridAggregateType.Sum">
                        <FooterTemplate Context="agg">Total: @agg.FormattedValue</FooterTemplate>
                    </BitDataGridColumn>
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Stock" Align="BitTextAlign.End" Comparer="stockComparer">
                        <Template Context="p">
                            <BitTag Text="@(p.Stock == 0 ? "Out of stock" : $"{p.Stock} in stock")"
                                    Color="@(p.Stock == 0 ? BitColor.Error : BitColor.Success)" />
                        </Template>
                    </BitDataGridColumn>
                    <BitDataGridColumn ColumnId="Value" Title="Value" Align="BitTextAlign.End"
                                       SortBy="@(p => p.Price * p.Stock)">
                        <Template Context="p">@((p.Price * p.Stock).ToString("C0"))</Template>
                    </BitDataGridColumn>
                </BitDataGrid>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="Detail rows" RazorCode="@example9RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example9CsharpCode" Id="example9">
                <div>
                    <b>DetailTemplate</b> renders anything under a row - here a nested grid. A row opens from its ▸ toggle, from a click on
                    the row (<b>ExpandDetailOnRowClick</b>) or from code; <b>ShowDetailToggle="false"</b> hides the toggle column,
                    <b>ExpandedDetailItems</b> is bindable and <b>OnDetailToggle</b> reports each change.
                    <br /><br />
                    With <b>ExpandDetailOnRowClick</b> a click also selects the row in Single <b>SelectionMode</b>; clicks on the toggle,
                    checkbox, reorder and command cells, and on a control inside a cell, open nothing. Several rows can be open at once.
                    Binding <b>ExpandedDetailItems</b> puts the open rows in your hands - the grid then only reports changes through
                    <b>ExpandedDetailItemsChanged</b>; leave it unset and drive the rows with <b>ExpandDetailAsync</b>,
                    <b>CollapseDetailAsync</b>, <b>ToggleDetailAsync</b>, <b>SetDetailExpandedAsync</b>, <b>ExpandAllDetailsAsync</b> (the
                    rows of the view - only the loaded ones with <b>OnRead</b>, an IQueryable or <b>OnLoadMore</b>) and
                    <b>CollapseAllDetailsAsync</b>, and read them with <b>IsDetailExpanded</b>. <b>OnDetailToggle</b> reports a single row's
                    toggle, with the row and whether it opened.
                    <br /><br />
                    A detail row is as tall as its content, which <b>Virtualize</b> cannot measure: pair detail rows with paging rather
                    than virtualization. A grid nested in a detail row is a grid of its own; give it an <b>AriaLabel</b> that says whose
                    rows it shows.
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitStack Horizontal Wrap VerticalAlign="BitAlignment.Center">
                    <BitButton OnClick="ExpandAllDetails">Expand all</BitButton>
                    <BitButton OnClick="CollapseAllDetails" Variant="BitVariant.Outline">Collapse all</BitButton>
                    <BitText>@detailStatus</BitText>
                </BitStack>
                <br />
                <BitDataGrid @ref="detailGrid" TItem="SupplierModel" Items="@suppliers" Height="520px"
                             ExpandDetailOnRowClick="true" OnDetailToggle="OnDetailToggled">
                    <DetailTemplate Context="supplier">
                        <BitDataGrid Items="supplier.Products" AriaLabel="@($"Products of {supplier.Name}")">
                            <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Id" Title="ID" Width="70px" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                            <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Name" Width="220px" />
                            <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Category" />
                            <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Price" Format="C2" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                        </BitDataGrid>
                    </DetailTemplate>
                    <Columns>
                        <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Name" Title="Supplier" Width="260px" />
                        <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.ProductCount" Title="Products" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                        <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.TotalStock" Title="Total stock" Format="N0" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                        <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.AveragePrice" Title="Avg price" Format="C2" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                    </Columns>
                </BitDataGrid>
            </DemoExample>
        </DemoTopic>

        <DemoTopic Title="Columns & styling" Route="columns"
                   Description="Resizing, reordering, hiding, freezing and grouping columns, column spans, borders, stripes and row numbers, row and cell styles with tooltips, text wrapping and row height, and the empty and loading states.">
            <DemoExample Title="Column layout" RazorCode="@example10RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example10CsharpCode" Id="example10">
                <div>
                    <b>Resizable</b>: drag a header's edge, double-click it to fit the content, or focus it and use the arrow keys (Home/End go
                    to <b>MinWidth</b>/<b>MaxWidth</b>, Enter fits). <b>Reorderable</b>: drag a header, press Ctrl+←/→ on it, or use the arrows in
                    the <b>ShowColumnChooser</b> panel, which also hides columns. Dragging works with touch and pen too. <b>Frozen</b> and
                    <b>FrozenEnd</b> pin columns to an edge, and <b>Group</b> banners adjacent columns under one header.
                    <br /><br />
                    <b>MinWidth</b> (60 px) and <b>MaxWidth</b> (none) bound the resize in pixels; Shift with an arrow key takes bigger
                    steps. <b>Resizable</b> and <b>Reorderable</b> on a column override the grid's, and a disabled grid does neither.
                    <b>AutoFitColumnAsync</b> and <b>AutoFitAllColumnsAsync</b> fit from code, measuring the rendered content and header;
                    <b>MoveColumnAsync</b> moves a column to a 0-based position.
                    <br /><br />
                    The column chooser shows and hides columns, and moves them only while the grid is <b>Reorderable</b>. A column's
                    <b>Visible</b> (default <code>true</code>) sets whether it starts shown. A hidden column is left out of the exports
                    and the clipboard too.
                    <br /><br />
                    <b>Frozen</b> pins to the start edge (the left in LTR, the right in RTL) and <b>FrozenEnd</b> to the end edge; a column
                    with both is Frozen. In the Excel export, the frozen columns become frozen panes. A <b>Group</b> header spans runs of
                    consecutive columns with the same name, so a column moved away from the others of its group no longer sits under it.
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitDataGrid Items="@columnsProducts" Height="460px"
                             Resizable="true" Reorderable="true" ShowColumnChooser="true">
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Id" Title="ID" Width="80px" Align="BitTextAlign.End" Frozen="true" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Name" Width="220px" MinWidth="120" MaxWidth="400" Frozen="true" Group="Identity" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Category" Width="160px" Group="Identity" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Supplier" Width="200px" Group="Identity" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Price" Width="160px" Format="C2" Align="BitTextAlign.End" Group="Commercials" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Stock" Width="140px" Align="BitTextAlign.End" Group="Commercials" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.ReleaseDate" Title="Released" Width="160px" Format="yyyy-MM-dd" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Rating" Width="110px" Format="N1" Align="BitTextAlign.End" FrozenEnd="true" />
                </BitDataGrid>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="Column spanning" RazorCode="@example11RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example11CsharpCode" Id="example11">
                <div>
                    <b>ColSpan</b> lets a cell cover the next columns of its row: a discontinued product's name spans Category, and a premium
                    price spans Stock. Assistive technologies get the span through <i>aria-colspan</i>.
                    <br /><br />
                    The function returns how many columns the cell covers, counted in the visible columns from its own;
                    <code>null</code>, 1 or anything below 1 covers just its own, and a span past the last column stops there. The covered
                    cells are not rendered, so their values do not show in that row - render what the row needs in the spanning cell's
                    <b>Template</b>, as here. Keyboard navigation steps over a span as one cell, and the Excel export writes it as a merged
                    region. Hiding or reordering columns changes what a span covers. While any column has a <b>ColSpan</b>,
                    <b>VirtualizeColumns</b> is not applied.
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitDataGrid Items="@spanningProducts" Height="460px">
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Id" Title="ID" Width="70px" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Name" Width="220px" ColSpan="p => NameSpan(p)">
                        <Template Context="p">
                            @if (p.Discontinued)
                            {
                                <span class="span-banner">⚠ @p.Name - discontinued</span>
                            }
                            else
                            {
                                @p.Name
                            }
                        </Template>
                    </BitDataGridColumn>
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Category" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Price" Format="C2" Align="BitTextAlign.End" ColSpan="p => PriceSpan(p)">
                        <Template Context="p">
                            @if (p.Price > 800)
                            {
                                <span class="span-banner">★ Premium: @p.Price.ToString("C2") · @p.Stock in stock</span>
                            }
                            else
                            {
                                @p.Price.ToString("C2")
                            }
                        </Template>
                    </BitDataGridColumn>
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Stock" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                </BitDataGrid>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="Borders, striping &amp; row numbers" RazorCode="@example12RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example12CsharpCode" Id="example12">
                <div>
                    <b>Bordered</b>, <b>Striped</b> and <b>Hoverable</b> are on by default. <b>ShowRowNumbers</b> numbers the rows by their place
                    in the whole data set - page 2 starts at 9 - and is never exported.
                    <br /><br />
                    <b>Bordered</b> draws the outer border and the lines between columns; the lines between rows are drawn either way.
                    <b>Striped</b> shades every other row and <b>Hoverable</b> highlights the row under the pointer. Their colors are the
                    <b>--bit-DataGrid-border-color</b>, <b>-stripe-background</b> and <b>-hover-background</b> CSS variables (see
                    <BitLink Href="/components/datagrid/localization#example31">Style &amp; Class</BitLink>). Row numbers keep counting
                    across pages, a virtualized window and infinite-scroll batches, and are left out of the clipboard as well as the exports.
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitStack Horizontal Wrap VerticalAlign="BitAlignment.Center">
                    <BitButton OnClick="() => bordered = !bordered">Borders: @(bordered ? "on" : "off")</BitButton>
                    <BitButton OnClick="() => striped = !striped">Striping: @(striped ? "on" : "off")</BitButton>
                    <BitButton OnClick="() => hoverable = !hoverable">Hover: @(hoverable ? "on" : "off")</BitButton>
                    <BitButton OnClick="() => rowNumbers = !rowNumbers">Row numbers: @(rowNumbers ? "on" : "off")</BitButton>
                </BitStack>
                <br />
                <BitDataGrid Items="@borderStripeProducts" Height="420px"
                             Bordered="bordered" Striped="striped" Hoverable="hoverable" ShowRowNumbers="rowNumbers"
                             Pageable="true" PageSize="8">
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Id" Title="ID" Width="70px" Align="BitTextAlign.End" Frozen="true" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Name" Width="220px" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Category" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Price" Format="C2" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Stock" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                </BitDataGrid>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="Row &amp; cell styling" RazorCode="@example13RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example13CsharpCode" CodeFiles="@example13CodeFiles" Id="example13">
                <div>
                    <b>RowClass</b> and <b>RowStyle</b> style a row from its data - out-of-stock rows turn red, premium ones bold;
                    a column's <b>CellClassSelector</b> and <b>CellStyleSelector</b> do the same for its own cells - low stock in amber.
                    <b>ShowCellTooltips</b> gives every cell its full text as a tooltip (a column opts out with <b>ShowTooltip="false"</b>):
                    narrow the Name column and hover a name.
                    <br /><br />
                    Each selector is called per row and returns <code>null</code> for nothing extra. <b>RowClass</b> is added after the
                    grid's own row classes and the <b>Classes.Row</b> / <b>SelectedRow</b> ones, and <b>RowStyle</b> after the row's layout
                    style. On a column, <b>CellClass</b> is a fixed class for every cell, <b>CellClassSelector</b> adds one per row after it,
                    and <b>CellStyleSelector</b>'s style is applied last, so it wins over the grid's own cell styles - prefer
                    <code>color</code> and <code>font-*</code> to <code>background</code>, which would hide the selected and hovered
                    states. <b>HeaderClass</b> styles the column's header cell.
                    <br /><br />
                    The classes land on elements the grid renders, so CSS isolation reaches them only through <code>::deep</code> from a
                    plain element of your page, which is why the grid sits in a <code>div</code> here (see the stylesheet tab). The Stock
                    column names its <code>TItem</code> because it is handed a method group, from which the compiler cannot infer the row
                    type. The tooltips only go on cells that show the column's own value - a <b>Template</b> draws its own.
                </div>
                <br />
                @* The row class lands on an element the grid renders, so the page's scoped CSS needs a
                   plain wrapper of its own to hang ::deep off (see the stylesheet tab). *@
                <div class="styled-rows">
                    <BitDataGrid Items="@styledRowProducts" Height="430px" Resizable="true"
                                 ShowCellTooltips="true"
                                 RowClass="RowClassFor" RowStyle="RowStyleFor">
                        <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Id" Title="ID" Width="70px" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                        <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Name" Width="160px" />
                        <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Supplier" Width="140px" />
                        <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Category" ShowTooltip="false" />
                        <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Price" Format="C2" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                        <BitDataGridColumn TItem="Product" Property="p => p.Stock" Align="BitTextAlign.End" CellStyleSelector="StockStyleFor" />
                    </BitDataGrid>
                </div>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="Text wrapping &amp; row height" RazorCode="@example14RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example14CsharpCode" Id="example14">
                <div>
                    <b>WrapCellText</b> wraps long values (and headers) instead of cutting them off, and the row grows to fit; a column overrides it
                    with <b>WrapText</b>. <b>RowHeight</b> (36) is every row's minimum height, and <b>RowHeightSelector</b> sets it per row - premium
                    products here. Wrapping is off while <b>Virtualize</b> is on, which needs every row at exactly RowHeight.
                    <br /><br />
                    Without wrapping, a value too long for its column is cut off with an ellipsis; <b>ShowCellTooltips</b> (in
                    <BitLink Href="#example13">Row &amp; cell styling</BitLink>) keeps it readable instead. <b>WrapText="false"</b> keeps a
                    column on one line while the grid wraps, as the ID, Price and Stock columns do here, and <b>WrapText="true"</b> wraps
                    one column of a grid that does not. Both heights are in pixels and are minimums: a row still grows to fit wrapped
                    text. While <b>Virtualize</b> is on, <b>RowHeightSelector</b> is ignored as well and <b>RowHeight</b> is every row's
                    exact height.
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitButton OnClick="() => wrapCellText = !wrapCellText">Wrapping: @(wrapCellText ? "on" : "off")</BitButton>
                <br /><br />
                <BitDataGrid Items="@wrapProducts" Height="430px" Resizable="true"
                             WrapCellText="wrapCellText" RowHeightSelector="RowHeightOf">
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Id" Title="ID" Width="70px" Align="BitTextAlign.End" WrapText="false" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Name" Width="160px" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn TItem="Product" Title="Description" Width="280px">
                        <Template Context="product">@DescriptionOf(product)</Template>
                    </BitDataGridColumn>
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Price" Format="C2" Width="100px" Align="BitTextAlign.End" WrapText="false" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Stock" Width="90px" Align="BitTextAlign.End" WrapText="false" />
                </BitDataGrid>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="Empty &amp; loading states" RazorCode="@example15RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example15CsharpCode" Id="example15">
                <div>
                    With no rows the grid shows a message, or your <b>EmptyTemplate</b>. <b>Loading</b> shows a spinner - or your
                    <b>LoadingTemplate</b> - in place of the rows and marks the grid <i>aria-busy</i>.
                    <br /><br />
                    Without an <b>EmptyTemplate</b> the message is <b>Strings.EmptyText</b> ("No items found") when there are no rows,
                    and <b>Strings.NoMatchesText</b> ("No results found") when a filter or the search is on - the rows exist but none
                    match. Both texts default to <b>BitStrings.Current</b>, read each time they are shown. An <b>EmptyTemplate</b> is
                    shown in both cases, so give it wording that fits a fruitless search as well, or leave it out.
                    <br /><br />
                    <b>Loading</b> hides the rows for as long as it is set rather than dimming them. A server grid (<b>OnRead</b>) does
                    not need it: it marks itself busy while it reads and shows the loading row only while it has no rows yet, keeping the
                    rows on screen while the next ones load - set <b>Loading</b> yourself to hide them then too. The header and toolbar
                    stay in place. <b>Classes.Empty</b> / <b>Classes.Loading</b> (and their <b>Styles</b>) style the two messages.
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitStack Horizontal Wrap>
                    <BitButton OnClick="() => emptyHasData = false">Clear data</BitButton>
                    <BitButton OnClick="SimulateLoading" Variant="BitVariant.Outline">Simulate loading</BitButton>
                </BitStack>
                <br />
                <BitDataGrid Items="@EmptyCurrent" Height="320px" Loading="emptyLoading">
                    <LoadingTemplate>
                        <BitSpinnerLoading Label="Fetching the latest products…" />
                    </LoadingTemplate>
                    <EmptyTemplate>
                        <BitStack HorizontalAlign="BitAlignment.Center">
                            <BitText Typography="BitTypography.H6">Nothing here yet</BitText>
                            <BitButton OnClick="() => emptyHasData = true">Load sample data</BitButton>
                        </BitStack>
                    </EmptyTemplate>
                    <Columns>
                        <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Id" Title="ID" Width="70px" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                        <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Name" Width="240px" />
                        <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Category" />
                        <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Price" Format="C2" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                    </Columns>
                </BitDataGrid>
            </DemoExample>
        </DemoTopic>

        <DemoTopic Title="Data & performance" Route="data"
                   Description="The data modes - eager and lazily loaded trees, row and column virtualization, server-side paging with OnRead, infinite scrolling, IQueryable sources - and CSV and Excel export.">
            <DemoExample Title="Tree data" RazorCode="@example16RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example16CsharpCode" Id="example16">
                <div>
                    <b>ChildrenSelector</b> turns <b>Items</b> into the roots of a tree (a <i>treegrid</i> whose rows carry their level and
                    expanded state). Sorting keeps the hierarchy, and a search or filter prunes it to the matching branches.
                    <b>TreeInitiallyExpanded</b> sets how it opens; <b>ExpandAllAsync</b> / <b>CollapseAllAsync</b> drive it from code.
                    With <b>CellNavigation</b>, → and ← open and close a node from its first cell, and ← on a child goes to its parent.
                    <br /><br />
                    The selector returns a node's direct children, or <code>null</code> or an empty list for a leaf. Nodes open collapsed
                    unless <b>TreeInitiallyExpanded</b> is set, and the first column carries the toggle and the indentation
                    (<b>--bit-DataGrid-tree-indent</b> per level). Give the tree a <b>KeyField</b>: it is how the grid tells nodes apart,
                    and the expanded nodes are not part of the <b>GetState</b> snapshot for that reason. A tree shows every row it holds,
                    so the pager and grouping are set aside, and the rows cannot be reordered by dragging.
                    <br /><br />
                    The second grid loads lazily: <b>OnLoadChildren</b> (or <b>ChildrenProvider</b>, which takes no cancellation token)
                    fetches a node's children the first time it opens and keeps them for later toggles, and <b>HasChildrenSelector</b> says
                    which unloaded nodes get a toggle - without it a node shows one only once its children are loaded, so none do at first.
                    Set only one of <b>ChildrenSelector</b>, <b>ChildrenProvider</b> and <b>OnLoadChildren</b>. A lazy tree cannot see what
                    it has not loaded, so it has no filter row and no search box, and <b>TreeInitiallyExpanded</b> does not apply to it.
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitStack Horizontal Wrap>
                    <BitButton OnClick="ExpandAll">Expand all</BitButton>
                    <BitButton OnClick="CollapseAll" Variant="BitVariant.Outline">Collapse all</BitButton>
                </BitStack>
                <br />
                <BitDataGrid @ref="treeGrid" Items="@fileRoots" Height="460px" KeyField="n => n.Id"
                             ChildrenSelector="n => n.Children" TreeInitiallyExpanded="true"
                             ShowSearchBox="true" Filterable="true" CellNavigation="true">
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Name" Width="320px" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Kind" Title="Type" Width="120px" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Size" Title="Size (bytes)" Format="N0" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Modified" Format="yyyy-MM-dd" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                </BitDataGrid>
                <br /><br />
                <div>
                    <b>OnLoadChildren</b> loads a node's children on its first expand (cached afterwards), with a token the grid cancels
                    once it no longer needs them (new <b>Items</b>, or the grid is gone); <b>ChildrenProvider</b> does the same without the token.
                    <b>HasChildrenSelector</b> tells an unloaded node whether to offer a toggle. Each folder below takes 600 ms to load.
                    Collapsing a node while it loads does not cancel the load, and the grid ignores the
                    <i>OperationCanceledException</i> a cancelled fetch throws.
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitDataGrid Items="@lazyTreeRoots" Height="360px" KeyField="n => n.Id"
                             OnLoadChildren="LoadChildrenAsync" HasChildrenSelector="IsFolder">
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Name" Width="280px" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Kind" Title="Type" Width="110px" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Size" Title="Size (bytes)" Format="N0" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Modified" Format="yyyy-MM-dd" />
                </BitDataGrid>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="Virtualization" RazorCode="@example17RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example17CsharpCode" Id="example17" PreventRenderForMcpClient>
                <div>
                    <b>Virtualize</b> renders only the rows in view (it needs a <b>Height</b>), and <b>VirtualizeColumns</b> does the same for
                    columns, which then need pixel widths. This grid has 42 columns; try 100,000 rows. A grouped view is virtualized
                    the same way, its group rows <b>RowHeight</b> tall like the rest.
                    <br /><br />
                    The scroll position is worked out from <b>RowHeight</b> (36 px by default), so every row is exactly that tall while
                    <b>Virtualize</b> is on: <b>RowHeightSelector</b> and <b>WrapCellText</b> are ignored, and an open detail row is not
                    accounted for - use paging with detail rows. Without a <b>Height</b> the grid has no viewport of its own to scroll.
                    With <b>OnRead</b> and no pager, the rows are read from your code window by window as they scroll in (see
                    <BitLink Href="#example18">Server-side data</BitLink>).
                    <br /><br />
                    <b>VirtualizeColumns</b> replaces the columns scrolled out of view with spacers. It places them from their pixel
                    widths - a column with another unit falls back to a default width - and is not applied while a column has a
                    <b>Group</b> header or a <b>ColSpan</b>. Keyboard navigation reaches only the columns that are rendered, and frozen
                    columns stay rendered.
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitStack Horizontal Wrap VerticalAlign="BitAlignment.Center">
                    <BitButton OnClick="() => virtualProducts = SampleData.Generate(1_000)">1k rows</BitButton>
                    <BitButton OnClick="() => virtualProducts = SampleData.Generate(10_000)">10k rows</BitButton>
                    <BitButton OnClick="() => virtualProducts = SampleData.Generate(100_000)">100k rows</BitButton>
                    <BitText>@virtualProducts.Count.ToString("N0") rows</BitText>
                </BitStack>
                <br />
                <BitDataGrid Items="@virtualProducts" Height="520px"
                             Virtualize="true" VirtualizeColumns="true">
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Id" Title="ID" Width="90px" Align="BitTextAlign.End" Frozen="true" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Name" Width="240px" />
                    @for (var m = 1; m <= 40; m++)
                    {
                        var month = m;
                        <BitDataGridColumn ColumnId="@($"m{month}")" Title="@($"M{month:00}")" Width="110px" Align="BitTextAlign.End">
                            <Template Context="product">@((product.Id * 37 + month * 13) % 1000)</Template>
                        </BitDataGridColumn>
                    }
                </BitDataGrid>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="Server-side data" RazorCode="@example18RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example18CsharpCode" Id="example18">
                <div>
                    <b>OnRead</b> hands sorting, filtering, searching and paging to your code: each request carries the page window, the
                    descriptors, the search term and a <i>CancellationToken</i> the grid cancels when a newer request replaces it. Return the
                    rows and the <b>TotalCount</b>.
                    <br /><br />
                    The request's <b>Skip</b> and <b>Take</b> are the window to return (<code>Take = null</code> means every row, which
                    the exports and <b>GetViewItemsAsync</b> ask for); <b>Sorts</b> come primary first, a row has to match every one of the
                    <b>Filters</b>, <b>Search</b> is the quick-search term to match across the columns you consider searchable, and
                    <b>Groups</b> names any grouping. Each filter names its column by id, an operator and a value - a collection for
                    <i>In</i> / <i>NotIn</i>, none for <i>IsEmpty</i> / <i>IsNotEmpty</i>. Pass the token on to your data source so the
                    work stops. <b>TotalCount</b> is the number of matching rows over every page and drives the pager; the result
                    refuses a total smaller than the rows it carries.
                    <br /><br />
                    In this mode the delegates of a column - <b>SortBy</b>, <b>Comparer</b>, <b>ValueText</b> - are not called: sort,
                    filter and search by the column ids yourself. Grouping is not offered. The footer aggregates only the page it holds
                    unless you compute the aggregates the request's <b>Aggregates</b> names over every matching row and return them in the
                    result's <b>Aggregates</b> (the second grid below). A read that throws propagates unless <b>OnReadError</b> is set,
                    which turns it into an error row with a Retry button (the third grid). The grid sets <i>aria-busy</i> while it reads, so <b>Loading</b> is only for a grid
                    that should hide its rows meanwhile. The grid reads only when what it asks for changes (a sort, a filter, the search,
                    the page or the page size), never on a re-render of its parent: after the data behind it changes, call
                    <b>RefreshAsync</b>.
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitDataGrid OnRead="LoadServerData" Height="430px" Loading="serverLoading"
                             Pageable="true" PageSize="10" Filterable="true">
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Id" Title="ID" Width="70px" Align="BitTextAlign.End" Filterable="false" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Name" Width="220px" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Category" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Supplier" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Price" Format="C2" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Stock" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                </BitDataGrid>
                <br />
                <BitText>@serverLastRequest</BitText>
                <br /><br />
                <div>
                    With <b>Virtualize</b> instead of paging, rows are fetched as they scroll into view - 100,000 here. The request's
                    <b>Aggregates</b> name what the footer shows (a sum of Price and an average of Rating), and the result's
                    <b>Aggregates</b> fill it in over the whole matching set.
                    The grid needs a <b>Height</b> and a fixed <b>RowHeight</b> here, as with any virtualized grid. A result with no
                    aggregates leaves the footer to aggregate the rows the grid holds; one that misses an aggregate the request named
                    shows none for that column. A column with its own <b>AggregateBy</b> is asked for as <i>Custom</i>, for the server to
                    compute its own way.
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitDataGrid OnRead="LoadVirtualServerData" Virtualize="true" Height="480px" RowHeight="40"
                             Filterable="true" ShowFooter="true">
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Id" Title="ID" Width="90px" Align="BitTextAlign.End" Frozen="true" Filterable="false" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Name" Width="240px" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Category" Width="140px" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Supplier" Width="180px" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Price" Width="140px" Format="C2" Align="BitTextAlign.End" Aggregate="BitDataGridAggregateType.Sum" AggregateFormat="C0" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Stock" Width="120px" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Rating" Width="110px" Format="N1" Align="BitTextAlign.End" FrozenEnd="true" Aggregate="BitDataGridAggregateType.Average" />
                </BitDataGrid>
                <br /><br />
                <div>
                    With <b>OnReadError</b> set, a read that throws stays inside the grid: an error row with a <b>Retry</b> button takes the
                    place of the rows, and you get the exception to log or report. Check the box, then page or sort - and uncheck it to
                    retry with success. Without <b>OnReadError</b> the exception propagates, as it always has.
                    A read the grid cancelled because a newer one replaced it is not a failure and raises nothing, and a failed export
                    throws to whatever started it rather than raising <b>OnReadError</b>. The texts are <b>Strings.ReadErrorText</b> and
                    <b>Strings.RetryText</b>, and <b>Classes.ReadError</b> styles the row.
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitCheckbox Label="Make the server fail" @bind-Value="serverFails" />
                <br />
                <BitDataGrid OnRead="LoadFlakyServerData" OnReadError="HandleReadError" Height="300px" Pageable="true" PageSize="5">
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Id" Title="ID" Width="70px" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Name" Width="220px" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Category" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Price" Format="C2" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                </BitDataGrid>
                <br />
                <BitText>@serverLastError</BitText>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="Infinite scrolling" RazorCode="@example19RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example19CsharpCode" Id="example19">
                <div>
                    <b>OnLoadMore</b> appends the next <b>LoadMoreBatchSize</b> rows as the user nears the end - no total, no pager - and stops at a
                    short batch. It needs a <b>Height</b>. An export asks once for every row with <i>Take = null</i>.
                    <br /><br />
                    Each request's <b>Skip</b> is the number of rows already loaded and its <b>Take</b> the batch size (50 by default),
                    with the sorts, filters and search as in server mode; return <code>0</code> as the <b>TotalCount</b>, which this mode
                    ignores. A batch shorter than <b>Take</b> tells the grid the data has ended. A change of sort, filter or search, and
                    <b>RefreshAsync</b>, start again from the first batch. The pager and grouping are not available, the footer
                    aggregates the loaded rows, and select-all, expand-all-details and the synchronous <b>ToCsv</b> /
                    <b>GetViewItems</b> cover only the loaded rows - <b>ToCsvAsync</b>, the export buttons and
                    <b>GetViewItemsAsync</b> read them all.
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitDataGrid OnLoadMore="LoadMore" LoadMoreBatchSize="40" Height="520px" RowHeight="40">
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Id" Title="ID" Width="90px" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Name" Width="240px" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Category" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Supplier" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Price" Format="C2" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Stock" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                </BitDataGrid>
                <br />
                <BitText>@infiniteLog</BitText>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="IQueryable data source" RazorCode="@example20RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example20CsharpCode" Id="example20">
                <div>
                    Bind an <b>IQueryable&lt;T&gt;</b> - an EF Core <i>DbSet</i>, say - and the filters, sorts, search and paging become an
                    expression the provider runs at the source, so only the current page is materialized.
                    <br /><br />
                    Only what can be written as an expression reaches the provider. The quick search becomes <i>Contains</i> conditions
                    over the text columns (an enum column's members are matched by their text), and the delegates of a column -
                    <b>SortBy</b>, <b>Comparer</b>, <b>ValueText</b> - are not used: a bound column sorts by its property and a
                    template-only column does not sort at all. Grouping is not offered, and a tree's <b>Items</b> are never treated as a
                    query. Select-all and expand-all-details cover the loaded page only.
                    <br /><br />
                    Like a server grid, a queryable one queries again only when what it asks for changes (a sort, a filter, the search,
                    the page), or when it is handed another <b>Items</b>; call <b>RefreshAsync</b> after the data behind it changes.
                    <b>OnViewChange</b> still works here, at the cost of a query of the whole view whenever it may have changed.
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitDataGrid Items="@queryableProducts" Height="430px"
                             Filterable="true" Pageable="true" PageSize="10">
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Id" Title="ID" Width="70px" Align="BitTextAlign.End" Filterable="false" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Name" Width="220px" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Supplier" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Price" Format="C2" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Stock" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                </BitDataGrid>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="Export" RazorCode="@example21RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example21CsharpCode" Id="example21">
                <div>
                    <b>ShowCsvExport</b> and <b>ShowExcelExport</b> download every matching row (not just the page) as CSV or a real .xlsx with
                    typed cells, an AutoFilter, frozen panes for <b>Frozen</b> columns and merged cells for <b>ColSpan</b>;
                    <b>ExcelExportStyled</b> adds the grid's current colors. <b>ExportValue</b> exports a template column's value,
                    <b>Exportable="false"</b> leaves a column out, and <b>ExportFileName</b> names the files. In code, every export method also
                    takes <i>selectedOnly</i>.
                    <br /><br />
                    An export follows the view - the filters, the search and the sorts - but not the page: it writes every matching
                    row, in every data mode (<b>OnRead</b> and <b>OnLoadMore</b> are asked once with <i>Take = null</i>). It writes the
                    visible columns that have a value, in their current order, with a header row of their titles; a template-only column
                    needs an <b>ExportValue</b>, which also lets a bound column export something other than its value. Values are
                    formatted with the column's <b>Format</b>; <b>ValueText</b> and templates are display only. The CSV guards against
                    formula injection by prefixing text that starts with =, +, - or @@ (numbers excepted) with an apostrophe.
                    <br /><br />
                    The .xlsx is built in-process, with no other package: numbers, booleans and dates land as typed cells, with the
                    column widths, an AutoFilter, frozen panes and merged <b>ColSpan</b> cells. <b>ExcelExportStyled</b> reads the
                    colors, striping, borders and fonts the grid renders (the active theme and dark mode included) and falls back to a
                    plain bold header where scripts cannot run, as while prerendering. <b>ExportFileName</b> defaults to
                    <i>export</i>. From code: <b>ExportCsvAsync</b>, <b>ExportExcelAsync</b> (download), <b>ToCsvAsync</b>,
                    <b>ToExcelAsync</b> (the text or bytes) and <b>ToCsv</b>, which is synchronous and covers only the loaded rows with
                    <b>OnRead</b> or <b>OnLoadMore</b>.
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitDataGrid Items="@exportProducts" Height="460px" Filterable="true"
                             ShowCsvExport="true" ShowExcelExport="true" ExcelExportStyled="true" ExportFileName="products">
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Id" Title="ID" Width="80px" Align="BitTextAlign.End" Frozen="true" Filterable="false" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Name" Width="220px" Frozen="true" ColSpan="p => NameSpan(p)">
                        <Template Context="p">
                            @if (p.Discontinued)
                            {
                                <span class="span-banner">⚠ @p.Name - discontinued</span>
                            }
                            else
                            {
                                @p.Name
                            }
                        </Template>
                    </BitDataGridColumn>
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Category" Width="170px" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Price" Width="150px" Format="C2" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.ReleaseDate" Title="Released" Width="130px" Format="d" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Supplier" Width="200px" Exportable="false" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn ColumnId="Value" Title="Value" Width="140px" Align="BitTextAlign.End"
                                       SortBy="@(p => p.Price * p.Stock)" ExportValue="@(p => p.Price * p.Stock)" Format="C0">
                        <Template Context="p">@((p.Price * p.Stock).ToString("C0"))</Template>
                    </BitDataGridColumn>
                </BitDataGrid>
                <br />
                <div>
                    <b>ToolbarIconOnly</b> renders the toolbar's buttons as icons for a compact toolbar - the exports, Columns, Clear
                    filters (once a filter is on) and Add row - their text kept as the accessible name and shown in a tooltip on hover,
                    focus and touch. A button with no icon of its own draws a built-in one; <b>CsvExportIconName</b>,
                    <b>ExcelExportIconName</b>, <b>ColumnChooserIconName</b>, <b>ClearFiltersIconName</b> and <b>AddRowIconName</b>
                    (or their <b>...Icon</b> twins for an external icon library) pick another, also shown beside the text when it is not
                    hidden.
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitDataGrid Items="@exportProducts" Height="300px" ShowSearchBox="true" Filterable="true" ShowColumnChooser="true"
                             ShowCsvExport="true" ShowExcelExport="true" ToolbarIconOnly="true" ExportFileName="products"
                             CsvExportIconName="@BitIconName.Download">
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Name" Width="220px" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Category" Width="170px" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Price" Width="150px" Format="C2" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                </BitDataGrid>
            </DemoExample>
        </DemoTopic>

        <DemoTopic Title="Interaction & state" Route="interaction"
                   Description="Dragging rows into a new order, row and cell events with a custom context menu, the programmatic API and its change events, and saving and restoring the grid's state.">
            <DemoExample Title="Row reordering" RazorCode="@example22RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example22CsharpCode" Id="example22">
                <div>
                    <b>RowReorderable</b> adds a ⠿ handle: drag it onto another row (mouse, touch or pen), or focus it and press ↑/↓. The grid
                    moves the item in the bound list and raises <b>OnRowReorder</b>. It pauses while the view is sorted, filtered or grouped.
                    <br /><br />
                    The move is made in place only when <b>Items</b> is a mutable list (<i>IList&lt;T&gt;</i>); otherwise
                    <b>OnRowReorder</b>'s <b>FromIndex</b> and <b>ToIndex</b> are <code>null</code> and the new order is yours to apply
                    from <b>DraggedItem</b> and <b>TargetItem</b>. Persist the order in the handler. Because a move is made by index in
                    the bound list, the handle also pauses while a search is on, and is not offered in a tree, with <b>OnRead</b>,
                    <b>OnLoadMore</b> or an IQueryable. That is why this grid sets <b>Sortable="false"</b>: a header click would sort the
                    rows and pause the handle. A disabled grid moves nothing.
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitDataGrid TItem="Product" Items="@reorderProducts" Height="460px" KeyField="p => p.Id"
                             RowReorderable="true" OnRowReorder="OnReorder" Sortable="false">
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Id" Title="ID" Width="70px" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Name" Width="220px" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Category" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Price" Format="C2" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                </BitDataGrid>
                <br />
                <BitText>@reorderLog</BitText>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="Cell events &amp; context menu" RazorCode="@example23RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example23CsharpCode" CodeFiles="@example23CodeFiles" Id="example23">
                <div>
                    <b>OnCellClick</b>, <b>OnCellDoubleClick</b> and <b>OnCellContextMenu</b> report the row, the column, the value and the mouse
                    event; right-click a cell for a custom menu. <b>OnRowClick</b> and <b>OnRowDoubleClick</b> report the row alone.
                    <br /><br />
                    The arguments carry the row (<b>Item</b>), the <b>Column</b> with its <b>ColumnId</b> and <b>ColumnTitle</b>, the cell's raw
                    <b>Value</b> (not its formatted text) and the <b>Mouse</b> event, whose coordinates place the menu here. While
                    <b>OnCellContextMenu</b> is set the browser's own menu is suppressed on the data cells.
                    <br /><br />
                    A click on a control inside a cell belongs to the control: a button, link or field of a template, an ARIA widget, an
                    element in the tab order, an element marked <code>data-bit-interactive</code>, the grid's own checkboxes and toggles, and
                    anything in a dialog or a grid inside the cell raise none of the row and cell events, select nothing and expand
                    nothing - and a right-click there keeps the browser's menu. Mark an element of a template
                    <code>data-bit-interactive</code> to treat it so (an icon with an <code>@@onclick</code>), or
                    <code>data-bit-interactive="false"</code> to let a control's clicks reach the cell and the row. A disabled grid raises
                    none of these events. The menu itself is the page's own markup (see the stylesheet tab); the grid only reports the
                    click.
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitDataGrid @ref="cellEventsGrid" TItem="Product" Items="@cellEventsProducts" Height="420px"
                             OnCellClick="OnCellClick"
                             OnCellDoubleClick="OnCellDoubleClick"
                             OnCellContextMenu="OnCellContextMenu">
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Id" Title="ID" Width="70px" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Name" Width="220px" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Category" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Price" Format="C2" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                </BitDataGrid>
                <br />
                <BitText>@cellEventStatus</BitText>

                @if (cellMenuArgs is not null)
                {
                    <div class="ctx-menu-overlay"
                         @onclick="CloseCellMenu"
                         @oncontextmenu="CloseCellMenu" @oncontextmenu:preventDefault="true"></div>
                    <div class="ctx-menu" role="menu" style="left:@(cellMenuX)px;top:@(cellMenuY)px"
                         @onkeydown="OnCellMenuKeyDown">
                        <div class="ctx-menu-header">@cellMenuArgs.Item.Name - @cellMenuArgs.ColumnTitle</div>
                        <button type="button" class="ctx-menu-item" role="menuitem" @ref="cellMenuFirstItem" @onclick="CopyCellValue">Copy value</button>
                        <button type="button" class="ctx-menu-item ctx-menu-danger" role="menuitem" @onclick="DeleteCellMenuRow">Delete row</button>
                    </div>
                }
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="Programmatic control" RazorCode="@example24RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example24CsharpCode" Id="example24">
                <div>
                    Everything the UI does is public API: <b>SortByAsync</b>, <b>ApplyFilterAsync</b>, <b>GroupByAsync</b>, <b>GoToPageAsync</b>,
                    <b>SetPageSizeAsync</b>, <b>MoveColumnAsync</b>, <b>SearchAsync</b>, <b>RefreshAsync</b> and more. <b>OnSortChange</b>,
                    <b>OnFilterChange</b>, <b>OnGroupChange</b> and <b>OnPageChange</b> report each change however it was made.
                    <b>OnViewChange</b> hands over the rows of the view across every page - what a map or a chart of the same rows needs -
                    and <b>RevealItemAsync</b> brings any of them into sight, going to its page and opening its group.
                    <br /><br />
                    The methods take a column by its id - the <b>ColumnId</b>, or the property path of a bound column (<code>Price</code>,
                    <code>Address.City</code>). <b>SortByAsync</b> replaces the sort unless <i>additive</i> is true, and
                    <i>BitDataGridSortDirection.None</i> removes the column's sort; <b>ApplyFilterAsync</b> replaces the column's filter,
                    <b>ApplyRangeFilterAsync</b> sets a from-inclusive, to-exclusive range; <b>GoToPageAsync</b> takes a 1-based page and
                    clamps it; <b>SetPageSizeAsync</b> and <b>SearchAsync</b> go back to the first page; <b>MoveColumnAsync</b> takes a
                    0-based position among all the columns. <b>ActiveSorts</b>, <b>ActiveFilters</b>, <b>ActiveGroups</b>,
                    <b>ActiveSearch</b>, <b>CurrentPage</b>, <b>TotalPages</b> and <b>TotalCount</b> read the current view.
                    <br /><br />
                    The change events fire for the user's changes and the API's alike, with the new descriptors (<b>OnPageChange</b> with the
                    1-based page, also when the page size changes). <b>OnViewChange</b> tells rows apart by <b>KeyField</b> (by reference
                    without one), so give it a key when the source hands over new instances of the same rows; it is raised with a list,
                    a tree or an IQueryable, while <b>OnRead</b> and <b>OnLoadMore</b> grids hold only a window of the view -
                    <b>GetViewItemsAsync</b> reads it all there, and <b>GetViewItems</b> returns the loaded rows. <b>RevealItemAsync</b>
                    returns <code>false</code> when the row is not in the view (filtered out, say). <b>RefreshAsync</b> recomputes the
                    view, or reads it again from a server.
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitStack Horizontal Wrap VerticalAlign="BitAlignment.Center">
                    <BitButton OnClick="ApiSortByPrice">Sort by price ↓</BitButton>
                    <BitButton OnClick="ApiClearSorts" Variant="BitVariant.Outline">Clear sorts</BitButton>
                    <BitButton OnClick="ApiFilterExpensive">Filter price &gt; 500</BitButton>
                    <BitButton OnClick="ApiClearFilters" Variant="BitVariant.Outline">Clear filters</BitButton>
                    <BitButton OnClick="ApiGroupByCategory">Group by category</BitButton>
                    <BitButton OnClick="ApiUngroup" Variant="BitVariant.Outline">Ungroup</BitButton>
                    <BitButton OnClick="ApiGoToPage3">Go to page 3</BitButton>
                    <BitButton OnClick="ApiPageSize50">Page size 50</BitButton>
                    <BitButton OnClick="ApiPriceFirst">Move Price first</BitButton>
                    <BitButton OnClick="ApiRevealRandom">Reveal a random row</BitButton>
                    <BitButton OnClick="ApiRefresh" Variant="BitVariant.Outline">Refresh</BitButton>
                </BitStack>
                <br />
                <BitDataGrid @ref="apiGrid" TItem="Product" Items="@apiProducts" Height="430px"
                             Filterable="true" Groupable="true" Pageable="true" PageSize="10"
                             OnSortChange="OnApiSortChange" OnFilterChange="OnApiFilterChange"
                             OnGroupChange="OnApiGroupChange" OnPageChange="OnApiPageChange"
                             OnViewChange="OnApiViewChange">
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Id" Title="ID" Width="70px" Align="BitTextAlign.End" Filterable="false" Groupable="false" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Name" Width="220px" Groupable="false" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Category" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Price" Format="C2" Align="BitTextAlign.End" Groupable="false" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Stock" Align="BitTextAlign.End" Groupable="false" />
                </BitDataGrid>
                <br />
                <BitText>@apiLog</BitText>
                <BitText>@apiViewLog</BitText>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="State persistence" RazorCode="@example25RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example25CsharpCode" Id="example25">
                <div>
                    <b>OnStateChange</b> hands over a <b>GetState</b> snapshot - page and page size, search, sorts, filters, groups and which are
                    open, and each column's order, width and visibility - after every change. Store it, then give it to <b>ApplyStateAsync</b>
                    on a new grid; recreate this one to see. A JSON round trip is fine: filter values are converted back to their column's type.
                    <br /><br />
                    The snapshot is a plain <b>BitDataGridState</b> - <b>CurrentPage</b>, <b>PageSize</b> (null while the
                    <b>PageSize</b> parameter applies), <b>Search</b>, <b>Sorts</b>, <b>Filters</b>, <b>Groups</b>, <b>GroupsCollapsed</b>
                    with the <b>GroupExpansionOverrides</b>, and a <b>BitDataGridColumnState</b> per column (<b>ColumnId</b>,
                    <b>Visible</b>, the resized <b>Width</b> and the <b>Order</b>) - so it serializes as it is, to local storage or a
                    user profile. It does not hold the selection, the open detail rows or the open tree nodes (a tree node is known only
                    by its <b>KeyField</b>, which need not serialize). Columns are matched by id, so a template-only column needs a
                    <b>ColumnId</b> of its own for its layout to come back.
                    <br /><br />
                    Apply the state once the grid has rendered and its columns have registered - from <b>OnAfterRenderAsync</b>, as here.
                    <b>ApplyStateAsync</b> does not raise <b>OnStateChange</b>; every other change does, the user's and the API's alike,
                    after the grid has re-rendered.
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitStack Horizontal Wrap VerticalAlign="BitAlignment.Center">
                    <BitButton OnClick="() => RecreateGrid(restore: true)" Disabled="savedGridState is null">Recreate &amp; restore</BitButton>
                    <BitButton OnClick="() => RecreateGrid(restore: false)" Variant="BitVariant.Outline">Recreate fresh</BitButton>
                    <BitText>@gridStateStatus</BitText>
                </BitStack>
                <br />
                <BitDataGrid @key="stateGridKey" @ref="stateGrid" TItem="Product" Items="@stateProducts" Height="430px"
                             Filterable="true" Resizable="true" Reorderable="true" ShowColumnChooser="true"
                             Pageable="true" PageSize="10" OnStateChange="SaveGridState">
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Id" Title="ID" Width="70px" Align="BitTextAlign.End" Filterable="false" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Name" Width="220px" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Category" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Price" Format="C2" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Stock" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                </BitDataGrid>
            </DemoExample>
        </DemoTopic>

        <DemoTopic Title="Localization & appearance" Route="localization"
                   Description="Translating the grid's texts and its values, the disabled grid, app-wide defaults through BitParams, the pager and toolbar layout, Style & Class with the CSS variables, and right-to-left grids.">
            <DemoExample Title="Localization" RazorCode="@example26RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example26CsharpCode" Id="example26">
                <div>
                    Every string the grid renders or announces - pager, messages, placeholders, buttons, accessible names and live-region
                    announcements - comes from <b>Strings</b>. Override what you need; the rest keeps its English default.
                    <br /><br />
                    <b>BitDataGridStrings</b> holds 95 texts. The ones with a <code>{0}</code> are format strings whose placeholders must be
                    kept (<b>PagerRangeFormat</b> takes the first row, the last row and the total). <b>EmptyText</b> and
                    <b>NoMatchesText</b> default to <b>BitStrings.Current</b>, so an app that localizes BitStrings already has them. Hand the
                    same instance to every grid of an app through <b>BitParams</b> (see
                    <BitLink Href="#example28">Cascading parameters</BitLink>).
                    <br /><br />
                    The column titles are yours: set <b>Title</b> in the user's language, since the default is the property name.
                    <b>Format</b> writes numbers and dates in the current culture (the Preis column's <code>C2</code> writes the page culture's currency), and
                    a cell's values themselves can be translated with <b>ValueText</b> (see
                    <BitLink Href="#example29">Value text</BitLink>).
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitDataGrid Items="@localizedProducts" Height="420px" Strings="@germanStrings"
                             ShowSearchBox="true" Filterable="true" Pageable="true" PageSize="8">
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Id" Title="Nr." Width="80px" Align="BitTextAlign.End" Filterable="false" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Name" Title="Name" Width="220px" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Price" Title="Preis" Format="C2" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Stock" Title="Bestand" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                </BitDataGrid>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="Disabled" RazorCode="@example27RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example27CsharpCode" Id="example27">
                <div>
                    <b>Disabled</b> keeps the data readable - a focused cell still moves and copies - but disables every control and
                    gesture in the grid, which reports <i>aria-disabled</i>.
                    <br /><br />
                    While disabled, the grid keeps showing its sort, filters, selection and page, but none of them can be changed from it:
                    headers do not sort, filters, the search box, checkboxes and the pager are disabled, columns cannot be resized or
                    moved, rows cannot be dragged, nothing opens for editing, and the row and cell events are not raised. Arrow keys
                    still move between the cells and Ctrl/⌘+C still copies. A <b>FilterTemplate</b> gets the state as its context's
                    <b>Disabled</b>, to disable its own control. The API methods are not blocked, so code can still sort, filter or page a
                    disabled grid. The text color is <b>--bit-DataGrid-disabled-color</b>.
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitToggle @bind-Value="gridDisabled" Label="Disabled" Inline />
                <br />
                <BitDataGrid Items="@disabledProducts" Height="380px" Disabled="gridDisabled"
                             SelectionMode="BitSelectionMode.Multiple" CellNavigation="true" ClipboardCopy="true"
                             Filterable="true" Resizable="true" Pageable="true" PageSize="8">
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Id" Title="ID" Width="70px" Align="BitTextAlign.End" Filterable="false" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Name" Width="220px" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Category" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Price" Format="C2" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                </BitDataGrid>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="Cascading parameters" RazorCode="@example28RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example28CsharpCode" Id="example28">
                <div>
                    <b>BitParams</b> carries a <b>BitDataGridParams</b> to every grid under it - the defaults an app wants everywhere,
                    <b>Strings</b> included. Both grids here take row numbers, a filter row and pages of 5 from it; they are defaults, not
                    overrides, so the second grid keeps the <b>Striped</b> it sets itself.
                    <br /><br />
                    A value set on the params object applies wherever the grid does not set the parameter itself; one left null leaves
                    the grid's own default. Put the <b>BitParams</b> high in the app - around the router in the layout - to make it
                    app-wide. Since the grid is generic over its rows, only what does not depend on them can be shared: the look
                    (<b>Bordered</b>, <b>Striped</b>, <b>Hoverable</b>, <b>RowHeight</b>, <b>Height</b>, <b>Classes</b>, <b>Styles</b>),
                    the features (<b>Filterable</b>, <b>Pageable</b>, <b>PageSize</b>, <b>Resizable</b>, <b>ShowSearchBox</b>, the export
                    buttons and their icons, ...), <b>Strings</b>, <b>EmptyTemplate</b> and <b>LoadingTemplate</b>. The data, the
                    selectors, the selection, the templates typed over the row, the event callbacks, and the choices that belong to one
                    data set - <b>Editable</b>, <b>SelectionMode</b>, <b>RowReorderable</b>, <b>Virtualize</b>,
                    <b>ToolbarTemplate</b>, <b>ExportFileName</b> - stay on each grid.
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitParams Parameters="@dataGridParams">
                    <BitDataGrid Items="@cascadingProducts" AriaLabel="Cascaded defaults">
                        <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Id" Title="ID" Width="70px" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                        <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Name" Width="220px" />
                        <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Price" Format="C2" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                    </BitDataGrid>
                    <br />
                    <BitDataGrid Items="@cascadingProducts" AriaLabel="Its own Striped" Striped="true">
                        <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Id" Title="ID" Width="70px" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                        <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Name" Width="220px" />
                        <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Price" Format="C2" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                    </BitDataGrid>
                </BitParams>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="Value text" RazorCode="@example29RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example29CsharpCode" Id="example29">
                <div>
                    <b>ValueText</b> sets the text a column's values are shown as - a translated or friendlier name for each member of an
                    enum, a word for a boolean - without changing the data. It receives a non-null value of the bound property and
                    returns its text, or <code>null</code> to keep the default: here four categories get a longer name and the others keep
                    their own, and the Discontinued flag reads as a status.
                    <br /><br />
                    The text is used wherever the grid shows the value: the cells (unless a <b>Template</b> draws them) and their tooltips,
                    the group headers (group by Category or Status), a Min or Max aggregate, the options of an enum or boolean column's
                    filter and inline editor, and, with an in-memory source, what the quick search and the text filter operators match -
                    search for "garden". It is display only: CSV, Excel and the clipboard keep writing the value formatted by
                    <b>Format</b> (give a column an <b>ExportValue</b> to export other text), sorting still orders the values themselves,
                    and an IQueryable or <b>OnRead</b> source searches and filters the stored value, except that an IQueryable's quick
                    search matches an enum column's members by their text.
                    <br /><br />
                    Without <b>ValueText</b> an enum member is shown by the name of its <code>[Display]</code> attribute (translated when
                    the attribute names a <i>ResourceType</i>), or else by its own name, and a <b>Format</b> writes the value instead
                    (<code>D</code> for an enum's number). A new text - another mapping, or a new UI culture - reaches the cells, group
                    headers and options on the next render; the search and the aggregates follow when the grid refreshes its view, which
                    handing over another method does, while the same method returning other text needs <b>RefreshAsync</b>.
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitDataGrid Items="@valueTextProducts" Height="430px"
                             ShowSearchBox="true" Filterable="true" Groupable="true" Pageable="true" PageSize="8">
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Id" Title="ID" Width="70px" Align="BitTextAlign.End" Filterable="false" Groupable="false" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Name" Width="220px" Groupable="false" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Category" Width="190px" ValueText="CategoryText" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Discontinued" Title="Status" Width="150px" ValueText="StatusText" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Price" Format="C2" Align="BitTextAlign.End" Groupable="false" />
                </BitDataGrid>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="Pager &amp; toolbar" RazorCode="@example30RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example30CsharpCode" Id="example30">
                <div>
                    <b>PagerPlacement</b> puts the pager below the rows (<i>Bottom</i>, the default), above them (<i>Top</i>) or both
                    (<i>TopAndBottom</i>); any other <b>BitPlacement</b> value is Bottom. <b>PageSizeOptions</b> are the sizes the pager's
                    dropdown offers (10, 20, 50 and 100 by default), and the <b>PageSize</b> in effect is added to them when it is not one
                    of them - 15 joins 10, 25 and 50 here. The pager only renders while paging is active: <b>Pageable</b> is set and the
                    rows are not grouped, a tree or infinitely scrolled. <b>OnPageChange</b> reports each new page.
                    <br /><br />
                    <b>ToolbarTemplate</b> puts your own content at the start of the toolbar, before the built-in controls (Add row, the
                    search box, Clear filters, the exports and Columns), and shows the toolbar on its own - here, buttons that move the
                    pager and hide the header. A toolbar with nothing in it takes no room. <b>Classes.Toolbar</b> and
                    <b>Classes.Pager</b> (and their <b>Styles</b>) style the two bars.
                    <br /><br />
                    <b>ShowHeader="false"</b> hides the header rows - the column titles, the <b>Group</b> headers and the filter row - for a
                    grid read as a plain list. Without headers there is nothing to click to sort, resize or reorder a column, so those are
                    left to the API (<BitLink Href="/components/datagrid/interaction#example24">Programmatic control</BitLink>).
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitDataGrid Items="@pagerProducts" Height="360px" ShowHeader="pagerShowHeader"
                             Pageable="true" PageSize="15" PageSizeOptions="pagerSizeOptions" PagerPlacement="pagerPlacement">
                    <ToolbarTemplate>
                        <BitStack Horizontal Wrap VerticalAlign="BitAlignment.Center">
                            <BitButton Size="BitSize.Small" Variant="@(pagerPlacement == BitPlacement.Top ? BitVariant.Fill : BitVariant.Outline)"
                                       OnClick="() => pagerPlacement = BitPlacement.Top">Pager on top</BitButton>
                            <BitButton Size="BitSize.Small" Variant="@(pagerPlacement == BitPlacement.Bottom ? BitVariant.Fill : BitVariant.Outline)"
                                       OnClick="() => pagerPlacement = BitPlacement.Bottom">At the bottom</BitButton>
                            <BitButton Size="BitSize.Small" Variant="@(pagerPlacement == BitPlacement.TopAndBottom ? BitVariant.Fill : BitVariant.Outline)"
                                       OnClick="() => pagerPlacement = BitPlacement.TopAndBottom">Both</BitButton>
                            <BitButton Size="BitSize.Small" Variant="BitVariant.Text"
                                       OnClick="() => pagerShowHeader = !pagerShowHeader">Header: @(pagerShowHeader ? "on" : "off")</BitButton>
                        </BitStack>
                    </ToolbarTemplate>
                    <Columns>
                        <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Id" Title="ID" Width="70px" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                        <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Name" Width="220px" />
                        <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Category" />
                        <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Price" Format="C2" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                    </Columns>
                </BitDataGrid>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="Style &amp; Class" RazorCode="@example31RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example31CsharpCode" CodeFiles="@example31CodeFiles" Id="example31">
                <div>
                    <b>Style</b> and <b>Class</b> land on the root; <b>Styles</b> and <b>Classes</b> reach each part on its own - the toolbar, the
                    header row and cells, the rows, a selected row, the cells, the footer, the pager and more.
                    <br /><br />
                    The parts are <b>Root</b>, <b>Toolbar</b>, <b>ColumnChooser</b>, <b>Viewport</b> (the scrolling area), <b>HeaderRow</b>,
                    <b>HeaderCell</b>, <b>FilterRow</b>, <b>Row</b>, <b>SelectedRow</b>, <b>Cell</b>, <b>GroupRow</b>, <b>DetailRow</b>,
                    <b>FooterRow</b>, <b>Pager</b>, <b>Empty</b>, <b>Loading</b> and <b>ReadError</b>. A part's class comes before the
                    per-row and per-column ones (<b>RowClass</b>, a column's <b>HeaderClass</b> and <b>CellClass</b>), which therefore win
                    at equal weight; <b>SelectedRow</b> is added after <b>Row</b>. The classes land on elements the grid renders, so a
                    page's isolated CSS reaches them through <code>::deep</code> from a plain element of its own - the
                    <code>div</code> around the first grid (see the stylesheet tab).
                </div>
                <br />
                <div class="styled-grid">
                    <BitDataGrid Items="@styleProducts" Height="300px" Style="box-shadow: var(--bit-shd-sm);"
                                 Classes="@(new() { HeaderCell = "custom-header-cell", SelectedRow = "custom-selected-row" })"
                                 Styles="@(new() { Root = "border-radius: 1rem;", Pager = "justify-content: center;" })"
                                 SelectionMode="BitSelectionMode.Multiple" @bind-SelectedItems="styleSelection"
                                 Pageable="true" PageSize="5">
                        <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Id" Title="ID" Width="70px" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                        <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Name" Width="220px" />
                        <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Price" Format="C2" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                    </BitDataGrid>
                </div>
                <br /><br />
                <div>
                    The grid also reads <BitLink Href="/components/datagrid/api#css-variables-section">CSS variables</BitLink>. They inherit, so a value on <i>:root</i> or
                    an ancestor restyles every grid below it, and one on <b>Style</b> restyles that grid - and any grid nested inside it, such
                    as one in a detail row. Here: a compact, accent-colored grid. The variables restyle the defaults of the theme: the
                    cell padding, font, header, stripe, hover, selected, editing, group, detail and footer colors, the accent of the sort
                    arrows and checkboxes, the focus color and the tree and group indents.
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitDataGrid Items="@styleProducts" Height="300px" ShowRowNumbers="true"
                             SelectionMode="BitSelectionMode.Multiple"
                             Style="--bit-DataGrid-cell-padding: 2px 8px;
                                    --bit-DataGrid-font-size: var(--bit-tpg-fs-xs);
                                    --bit-DataGrid-header-background: var(--bit-clr-pri);
                                    --bit-DataGrid-header-color: var(--bit-clr-pri-text);
                                    --bit-DataGrid-selected-background: var(--bit-clr-suc-tint);
                                    --bit-DataGrid-accent-color: var(--bit-clr-suc);
                                    --bit-DataGrid-border-radius: 0;">
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Id" Title="ID" Width="70px" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Name" Width="220px" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Category" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Price" Format="C2" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                </BitDataGrid>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="RTL" RazorCode="@example32RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example32CsharpCode" Id="example32" PreventRenderForMcpClient>
                <div>
                    <b>Dir="BitDir.Rtl"</b> mirrors the grid: frozen columns, arrows and keyboard navigation follow the reading direction.
                    Without it, the grid takes a cascaded <b>BitDir</b>, or else the page's direction.
                    <br /><br />
                    In a right-to-left grid the start edge is the right: <b>Frozen</b> columns pin to the right and <b>FrozenEnd</b> ones to
                    the left, <b>Align</b>'s Start and End swap sides (Left and Right do not), and the horizontal arrow keys - cell navigation, a tree node's open and close, a resize handle - follow
                    the reading direction. Pair it with translated <b>Strings</b> and column titles, as here; the category text comes from a
                    <b>Template</b>, where <b>ValueText</b> would also translate the filter options, the group headers and the search.
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitDataGrid Items="@rtlProducts" Height="420px" Dir="BitDir.Rtl" Strings="@persianStrings"
                             Resizable="true" Pageable="true" PageSize="8">
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Id" Title="شناسه" Width="90px" Align="BitTextAlign.End" Frozen="true" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Name" Title="نام" Width="220px" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Category" Title="دسته‌بندی">
                        <Template Context="product">@CategoryFa(product.Category)</Template>
                    </BitDataGridColumn>
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Price" Title="قیمت" Format="C2" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                    <BitDataGridColumn Property="p => p.Stock" Title="موجودی" Align="BitTextAlign.End" />
                </BitDataGrid>
            </DemoExample>
        </DemoTopic>

    </Examples>
